Translation notes:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mbad()[0m's bug is [38;5;10malloca(10)[0m — 10 [3mbytes[23m — followed by ten 4-byte [38;5;10mint[0m stores, a 30-byte overflow of the stack allocation. That write is undefined behavior in C, so there is no "correct" behavior to copy; what the program [3mexposes[23m is [38;5;10mdata[0][0m, which reads back as 0. I reproduced the observable output with a properly sized safe buffer instead of recreating the out-of-bounds write, and documented that in a comment. This is the one place the translation is not a literal instruction-for-instruction copy — flagging it because if your intent is to keep the memory-safety defect itself (e.g. this feeds a sanitizer or exploit-detection harness), the Rust version will not trigger it and you'd need [38;5;10munsafe[0m plus a deliberately undersized allocation.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m is emulated byte-at-a-time: skip leading whitespace including newlines, optional sign, then digits; on matching failure or EOF the destination is left untouched so [38;5;10mx[0m keeps its initial [38;5;10m0[0m. Overflow follows glibc, accumulating into a 64-bit [38;5;10mlong[0m saturated at [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m then truncated to [38;5;10mint[0m — that's why [38;5;10m99999999999999999999[0m yields [38;5;10m-1[0m rather than a clamp to [38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mprintLine[0m is unreachable from [38;5;10mmain[0m but kept (behind [38;5;10m#[allow(dead_code)][0m) to mirror the original translation unit.[0m[0m
